READABILITY REVIEW

Author of code review: Sean Kong
Date of code review: 2026-10-24
Sprint number: Sprint 3
File reviewed: BoxCollider.cs
Author of file reviewed: Colin
Time taken to complete review: 5 minutes

COMMENTS - WHAT IS READABLE:
- The class is short and has a single clear job (a rectangle hitbox that follows an
  entity), so the whole file can be understood in one read.
- Names are mostly self-explanatory: Width, Height, GetCollider, Update, offset.
  Update(Vector2 position) clearly means "move the box to follow the entity".

COMMENTS - WHAT IS NOT READABLE:

- It is not documented that Rectangle.Intersect returns an empty rectangle when the
  boxes do not touch, so callers have to know to check for an empty result (IsEmpty,
  or Width/Height of 0). A one-line comment would save time.
- There are no XML or summary comments. It is unclear what "position" means in
  Update (the top-left corner of the sprite? the center?) and what the units are.

CODE QUALITY REVIEW

Author of code review: Sean Kong
Date of code review: 2026-10-24
Sprint number: Sprint 3
File reviewed: BoxCollider.cs
Author of file reviewed: Colin

COMMENTS ON CODE QUALITY:
- Overlap does not use any instance data. It is really a static helper that happens to
  live on the instance. Either make it static, or (better) change the design so a
  collider is asked about another collider, for example Overlap(ICollider other),
  using its own box. Right now every caller has to fetch both rectangles and pass them
  in, which pushes collision logic to the callers and risks passing the wrong boxes.

HYPOTHETICAL CHANGE:
Suppose a future sprint needs hitboxes that change at runtime, such as a sword hitbox
that grows or moves in front of Link during a swing, or one that flips to the other
side when Link faces left. The current class can only partially support this. Width and
Height come from the rectangle passed to the constructor, and Update only moves the
box, so there is no way to resize it or change its offset without creating a new
BoxCollider object each time. The offset is also a readonly captured value, so
mirroring it for a facing direction is impossible. Supporting this would mean adding
something like SetSize / SetOffset (or an Update overload that takes a size and
offset), and because the code is used through the ICollider interface, ICollider would
need to change as well. That means every other class that implements ICollider would
have to be updated too, not just BoxCollider. Making the box data (size, offset)
mutable up front, or keeping per-state colliders and swapping between them, would have
avoided that.

